From 43a81800b8ed69568a1141a8793b88f0718a59bb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 12:00:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] Wayland: tell the person when a window asked for two
 views and got one

A window that asks for two views of its surface, one per eye (its own
stereo buffers, or an engine drawing the depth of its elements), and is
given one is shown flat. Until now that was a warning in the program's
log on the Vulkan path and nothing on the OpenGL path, which reaches no
one on a desktop.

Where the platform knows both what was asked and what the surface has,
it now hands the failure to the edition's problems engine
(sparky-stereo-problems): the Wayland Vulkan window when the swapchain's
maker says the views it got, and the Wayland EGL window when it makes its
surface or the request changes at the start of a frame. A driver that
cannot give a second view gives none to any program, so the problem is
the driver's, one for the person: kept once, naming the first program
that met it and the driver as its API names it (the Vulkan physical
device, which the swapchain's maker now says before the views with
"_q_stereo_surface_device"; the EGL vendor, or zink when the session
asks for it by name), and ended when a window gets its two views from
the same driver. Nothing is said when the pair works or when the window
never asked, and only where the compositor takes such a pair.

The engine's library is opened at run time, never linked, from the path
the build gives (QT_SPARKY_STEREO_PROBLEMS_LIBRARY); where it cannot be
loaded the engine's command keeps the same record. Without that path the
problem stays a warning in the program's log, as before. The messages
are the package's own, in the ten languages of its catalog.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5
